<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdullahi Issa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Abdullahi Issa Mohamud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdullahi Issa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-youth-league">Somali Youth League</a>. In 1956 he became the first prime minister of the Trust Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, the former Italian colony administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trusteeship, and he led its government until independence in 1960. He then served as foreign minister of the Somali Republic, formed by the union of the trust territory with British Somaliland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdullahi-issa.jpg" alt="Abdullahi Issa" width="199" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdullahi_Issa_Mohamud.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
